  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the investigation trail behind the file selection:\n\n1. **`pkg/registry/core/rest/storage_core.go`** \u2014 the exact source of both log lines. `legacyProvider.PostStartHook()` registers `\"start-service-ip-repair-controllers\"` and returns the literal error `\"unable to perform initial IP and Port allocation check\"` when `wg.Wait()` (both `startServiceClusterIPRepair` and `startServiceNodePortsRepair` reporting first-success via `onFirstSuccess`) doesn't complete within 1 minute. `New()` also shows the version-conditional branch: with `MultiCIDRServiceAllocator` enabled it wires `serviceipallocatorcontroller.NewRepairIPAddress(...).RunUntil`, the new IPAddress-object-based repair path.\n\n2. **`pkg/registry/core/service/ipallocator/controller/repairip.go`** \u2014 `RepairIPAddress.RunUntil` is where the readiness gets stuck: after cache sync, it polls (unbounded, no timeout other than the caller's 1-minute wall clock) for the default `ServiceCIDR` object via `serviceCIDRLister.Get(\"kubernetes\")` before running `runOnce()` and signaling `onFirstSuccess()`. `doRunOnce`/`syncService` is also what issues the `IPAddresses().Create(...)` calls that can surface as the `ipaddresses.networking.k8s.io \"\u2026\" is forbidden` errors during this window.\n\n3. **`pkg/controlplane/controller/defaultservicecidr/default_servicecidr_controller.go`** \u2014 the other half of the race: `Controller.Start` itself blocks apiserver startup polling until it can create/observe the default `ServiceCIDR` object and mark it `Ready=True`. This runs as a separate PostStartHook (`start-kubernetes-service-cidr-controller`, registered in `pkg/controlplane/instance.go`) with no ordering guarantee relative to the IP-repair hook \u2014 during a mixed-version rolling upgrade, one apiserver's write of the default ServiceCIDR/Ready-condition racing another node's repair-controller read is the mechanism that produces the transient readyz failure.\n\n4. **`pkg/registry/core/service/ipallocator/{cidrallocator,ipallocator,interfaces}.go`** \u2014 show the `Allocator.ready` atomic flag, `MetaAllocator.syncAllocators` gating readiness on the ServiceCIDR's `Ready` status condition, and `ErrNotReady`, which is the deeper reason writes get rejected until the default ServiceCIDR is observed as ready.\n\n5. **`repairip_test.go`** and **`pkg/registry/networking/ipaddress/strategy.go`** included for corroboration/context on the controller's construction and the (unguarded) IPAddress admission path.",
